h statement is a common mistake when working with radio news?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named spoken lead and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name spoken lead and explain why it matters only, without linking it to radio news","Apply radio news to new material and name the spoken lead and explain why it matters used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Reading a print article unchanged on air"],"question":"Which response best applies radio news with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The skill is shown by quoting or pointing to wording that carries radio news. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Ignore the sentence and invent a more exciting event","Say the writing is good without naming any words","Reading a print article unchanged on air","Point to exact words in the sentence that show radio news and say what they do"],"question":"Read: \"After the blackout, Tess lit a candle and read the crumpled note.\" Which choice best uses radio news from this lesson?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the language skill with textual evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Reading a print article unchanged on air","Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged","Name spoken lead and explain why it matters without linking it to any words in the text","Ear-friendly; complete lead; pauses sensible"],"question":"Which statement correctly applies the lesson idea about radio news?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Start from a specific textual clue, then explain its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Locate precise textual evidence, then explain how language choices shape meaning or effect","Guess from the title alone with no textual clue","Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged","Lengthen the answer with adjectives while keeping the same idea"],"question":"In a new text, what should you do first to analyse spoken lead and explain why it matters?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Repair means fixing meaning or evidence, not padding. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Make the answer longer while keeping the same mistake","Delete the evidence that disagreed with your first guess","Change the inaccurate idea or missing evidence, not only the length","Add adjectives without fixing the wrong idea"],"question":"Which revision actually improves meaning for radio news?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Accurate transfer needs fresh material with an explained language choice. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Change fonts and claim the work is new","Reading a print article unchanged on air","Use a fresh sentence or text and explain one precise choice about radio news","Retype the worked model as the final answer"],"question":"Which response shows accurate use of radio news on fresh material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about radio news with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating radio news on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using radio news on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.